According to the question, we can draw the following diagram
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fwbjee%252F2019_phy_a28.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2019_phy_a28.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2019_phy_a28.png?alt=media">
Given, $u=-0.2 \mathrm{m}$ and $f=0.05 \mathrm{m}$
As we know that,

\begin{array}{l}
\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{v}-\frac{1}{u} \\
\frac{1}{v}=\frac{1}{f}+\frac{1}{u} \\
\frac{1}{v}=\frac{1}{0.05}-\frac{1}{0.2} \\
\frac{1}{v}=\frac{100}{5}-\frac{10}{2} \\
\frac{1}{v}=20-5 \Rightarrow v=\frac{1}{15} \mathrm{m}
\end{array}

Now, differentiating eq. (i), we get

\begin{array}{l}
-\frac{d v}{v^{2}}=-\frac{d u}{u^{2}} \quad \therefore \quad d v=d u \frac{v^{2}}{u^{2}} \\
A_{\max }=A \times\left(\frac{1}{15}\right)^{2} \times\left(\frac{1}{(-0.2)}\right)^{2} \\
A_{\max }=A \times \frac{1}{225} \times 25 \\
A_{\max }=\frac{A}{9}
\end{array}

Here $A$ is in $\mathrm{cm} .$
Hence, $A_{\max }=\frac{A}{9} \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{m}$

The given combination of lenses
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fwbjee%252F2018_phy_a4.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2018_phy_a4.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2018_phy_a4.png?alt=media">

Here, $f_{1}=$ focal length of equiconvex lenses of glass. $f_{2}=$ focal length of lens formed by water (concave). The focal length of the combination.

\therefore \quad \frac{1}{F}=\frac{1}{f_{1}}+\frac{1}{f_{2}}+\frac{1}{f_{3}}=\frac{1}{f_{1}}-\frac{1}{f_{2}}+\frac{1}{f_{1}}=\frac{2}{f_{1}}-\frac{1}{f_{2}}

\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{F}=\frac{2 f_{2}-f_{1}}{f_{1} f_{2}} \Rightarrow F &=\frac{f_{1} f_{2}}{2 f_{2}-f_{1}} \\
F &=\frac{f_{1}}{2-\frac{f_{1}}{f_{2}}}
\end{aligned}

$\left[\because f_{3}=f_{1}\right]$
Here, $\frac{1}{f_{1}}=\left(\mu_{g}-1\right) \frac{2}{R},$ for $L_{1}$ and $L_{3}$
and $\frac{1}{f_{2}}=\left(\mu_{w}-1\right)\left(-\frac{2}{R}\right),$ for $L_{2}$
\text { Given, } \mu_{w} < \mu_{g}

Thus, $\frac{f_{1}}{f_{2}} \lt  1$
So.

F>\frac{f_{1}}{2}

From Eqs.
(i) and (ii), we get

\frac{f_{1}}{2} < F < f_{1} \quad \text { or } \frac{f}{2} < F < f

Given, radius $R=0.05 \mathrm{m}$ 
Refractive index $n=1 \cdot 5$
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fwbjee%252F2017_phy_a21.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2017_phy_a21.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2017_phy_a21.png?alt=media">

The critical angle, $\sin c=\frac{1}{n}=\frac{1}{1 \cdot 5}$
=\frac{10}{15}=\frac{2}{3}

From above figure, $\cos c=\frac{R}{R+X}\sqrt{1-\sin ^{2} c}=\frac{R}{R+X}\sqrt{1-\frac{4}{9}}=\frac{R}{R+X}\therefore$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3} &=\frac{R}{R+X} \\
\sqrt{5}(R+X) &=3 R \\
\sqrt{5} R+\sqrt{5} X &=3 R \\
X &=(3 \sqrt{5}-5) \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{m}
\end{aligned}

Here, angle of incidence is $60^{\circ}$
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fwbjee%252F2016_phy_a8.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2016_phy_a8.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fwbjee%2F2016_phy_a8.png?alt=media">


\mu=\frac{\sin i}{\sin r}

$\sin i=\mu \sin r$
here,

\begin{aligned}
r &=90-i \\
\sin i &=\mu \sin \left(90^{\circ}-i\right)=\mu \cos i \\
\mu &=\frac{\sin i}{\cos i}=\tan i=\tan 60^{\circ} \\
\mu &=\sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}

Thickness of glass plate $=t$
Refractive index $=\mu$ 
Velocity of light = c

\begin{array}{r}
\text { Speed of light }=\frac{\text { distance }}{\text { time }}=\frac{t}{T} \\
\qquad T=\frac{t}{\text { speed of light }}
\end{array}

Speed of light in glass plate

\begin{array}{l}
v=\frac{c}{\mu} \\
T=\frac{t}{c / \mu}=\frac{\mu t}{c}
\end{array}

\left(\because \mu=\frac{c}{v}\right)

For the first condition
